            <div xml:id="d68">

                <pb facs="0138" n="64" sameAs="frus:frus1866p2/pg64"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Sanford to Mr.
                        Seward.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#float-left">No. 357.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Brussels,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1866-05-03"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >May</hi> 3, 1866</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: General Baron de Beaulieu has returned here
                    from Washington, and both he and Mr. Rogier express great satisfaction at the
                    cordial, friendly reception given to the special mission by the President and
                    yourself; and the facilities extended to the officers of his suite by the
                    Departments of War and of the Navy, in obtaining information of cur progress in
                    the sience of war, have also given great gratification. The courtesies which the
                    mission has received seem to be very warmly appreciated, and will help to
                    increase that cordiality of feeling towards the United States which, I am happy
                    to say, exists with and on the part of the government and people.</p>
                <p>General Fourey, who went on a special mission to Mexico, as before reported to
                    you, also returned with Baron de Beaulieu with a part of his suite. On the 4th
                    of March, on their way from Mexico to Vera Cruz, he and his suite were attacked
                    at Rio Frio. One of his officers, Baron d’Huart, attached to the service of the
                    Count de Flandre, was killed; the general himself slightly, and some of his
                    staff severely wounded, necessitating their return to Mexico.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">* * * * * *</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, with great respect, your most obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>H. J. SANFORD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward,</hi>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
